[
  {
    "id": 15,
    "latex": "Now assume for reductio that there is an argument satisfying conditions (i)-(v). Let $P_{1}, P_{2}, \ldots$ be the premises of this argument, and let $C$ be its conclusion. Let $w$ be the actual world. Then we have the following:"
  },
  {
    "id": 16,
    "latex": "1. $P_{1}, P_{2}, \ldots$ entail $C$. (Condition (iii).)"
  },
  {
    "id": 17,
    "latex": "2. $C$ is non-trivial and evaluative in $w$. (Conditions (iv) and (v).)"
  },
  {
    "id": 18,
    "latex": "3. There exist value systems $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ such that $(w \wedge v_{1})$ entails $C$ and $(w \wedge v_{2})$ entails $\neg C$. (From 2.)"
  },
  {
    "id": 19,
    "latex": "4. $\neg C$ entails that at least one of $\{P_1, P_2, \ldots\}$ is false. (From 1.)"
  },
  {
    "id": 20,
    "latex": "5. $(w \wedge v_{2})$ entails that at least one of $\{P_1, P_2, \ldots\}$ is false. (From 3, 4.)"
  },
  {
    "id": 21,
    "latex": "6. There is a specific premise in $\{P_1, P_2, \ldots\}$, call it $P_i$, such that $(w \wedge v_2)$ entails $\neg P_i$. (From 5 and Determinacy.)"
  },
  {
    "id": 22,
    "latex": "7. $w$ conjoined with the correct value system entails $P_{i}$. (From condition (i) and Determinacy.)"
  },
  {
    "id": 23,
    "latex": "8. $P_{i}$ is evaluative in $w$. (From 6, 7.)"
  },
  {
    "id": 24,
    "latex": "Conclusion (8) follows from (6) and (7) because, given $w$, the truth of $P_{i}$ turns on what the correct value system is—$P_{i}$ is true according to the correct value system but would be false according to $v_{2}$. But (8) contradicts condition (ii). Thus, there is no argument satisfying all of conditions (i)-(v)."
  }
]
